<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">ʻOro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ʻOro was a god of war and fertility in the religion of the Society Islands in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, said to be a son of the creator Taʻaroa. His cult spread from the great marae of Taputapuātea on Raiatea to Tahiti and beyond in the 17th and 18th centuries, carried by chiefs and by the Arioi society of performers. It declined after Christian conversion in the 1810s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oro.jpg" alt="ʻOro" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: American at de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tahiti-Oro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Society Islands shared four main gods with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>: Taʻaroa, first a god of the sea and fishing, Tane of the forest and crafts, Tu, the old god of war, and Roʻo of crops and weather. On Raiatea the priests raised Taʻaroa to the creator of the world, perhaps because the ariki, the highest hereditary chiefs there, traced their descent directly from him. The worship of ʻOro, son of Taʻaroa and Hina tu a uta, grew out of this cult; tradition makes the marae <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taputapuatea">Taputapuatea</a> in the Opoa valley, which is dedicated to him, his birthplace.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taputapuatea">Taputapuatea</a> became a kind of central place of pilgrimage, ʻOro gained political and religious weight. On Tahiti his worship grew in the late proto-historical or early historical period, driven above all by the Arioi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> from whose ranks came the upper nobility and priesthood and which traced its own foundation to ʻOro. On the Marquesas he was called Mahui, and on many other islands he never held the same place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>On Tahiti ʻOro was the main god and the god of war, who in peacetime became the god of the fine arts. He showed himself as an ata, a natural object such as a bird, in two forms: ʻOro of the yellow belt, a light yellow thrush, and ʻOro of the red belt, a red-green bird. His toʻo, the image made by people, was an effigy wrapped in coconut fibre around a mace-shaped wooden soul, with red and yellow feathers, his symbols, laid between the layers; kept on the ceremonial platform, it was clothed again in tapa in a complex rite.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Pigs and also human beings were sacrificed to him. On his third voyage, in 1777, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> saw such a sacrifice, in which a priest killed a prisoner held on a platform by smashing his skull with a sacred mace. In the legends ʻOro lived with his sisters Teouri and Oaaoa on Mount Pahia on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora</a>, came down on a rainbow as a warrior to find a wife, and married Vairaumati, whom he made a goddess and who bore a son destined to be a powerful chief.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ʻOro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
